From 54fd62552ba2af92827b2cbc821457bae7d0964c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: knfsd-fuzz <knfsd-fuzz@localhost>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 20:20:51 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] sunrpc: attribute lane NFS server work to the lane's
 executor

The NFS fuzzing environment runs one NFS lane per executor proc: a server
network namespace and client namespaces that only that proc uses.  Proc N,
whose KCOV common handle is N + 1, is bound to lane N.  Every request that
reaches lane N's server therefore belongs to the program proc N is running.

Derive ownership from the server netns instead of from the client
connection.  Lane namespaces join a lane domain through debugfs
sunrpc_fuzz/domain_control ("create LANE" in the server netns, "join LANE
EPOCH" in each client netns, "retire LANE EPOCH").  When svc_tcp_recvfrom()
completes a C2S record in a lane netns, the request is given a token of the
generation currently open for handle lane + 1; svc_process() admits it and
runs the remote section under that generation.  A record that arrives while
no generation is open, or whose generation is already closing, stays
ownerless, which fences work issued after a program ended.  Deferred
requests keep their attribution through svc_defer()/svc_deferred_recv().

Because ownership does not depend on the TCP connection, it survives relays
that terminate TCP between client and server and mutations that change
records on the wire.

A nested KCOV section quarantines the lane: the lane is marked unhealthy, its
work stays ownerless, and the server stream is closed so that a hard-mounted
client reconnects instead of waiting for a dropped reply.

The NFS server data-ready callback and svc_recv() dequeue are recorded with
the transport observation handle when KCOV is enabled.

sunrpc.lane_attribution=0 disables the attribution (default on).  debugfs
sunrpc_fuzz/{lane_stats,saved_work_stats,lane_state} report its counters.
